# 03 — Embeddings & Vector Databases

⬅ [[00-START-HERE]]

## Why this matters
Embeddings are the foundation every RAG system is built on. "How do I return an item?" and "What's your refund policy?" land close together in vector space despite sharing no keywords — that's the entire trick that makes semantic search possible.

## Core Concepts (read once)
- **Embeddings** — a model converts text into a fixed-length vector of numbers capturing semantic meaning. Similar meaning → vectors close together in that space.
- **Similarity metrics** — cosine similarity is the default for text embeddings; dot product and Euclidean exist but cosine is what you'll use 95% of the time.
- **Chunking strategy** — you can't embed a whole document as one vector and expect good retrieval. Fixed-size chunking (e.g. 500 tokens, 50 overlap) is the baseline; semantic/recursive chunking (split on natural boundaries — paragraphs, headers) is better but more work. Chunk size is one of the highest-leverage tuning knobs in RAG.
- **Vector databases** — purpose-built for fast similarity search at scale (you can't linear-scan millions of vectors).
  - **Chroma** — simplest, great for local dev/prototyping
  - **FAISS** — Meta's library, fast, in-process, no server needed
  - **Qdrant** — production-grade, filtering + hybrid search support, can self-host
  - **Pinecone** — managed/hosted, zero ops, costs money at scale
- **Metadata filtering** — store metadata (date, source, tags) alongside vectors so you can filter *before or after* the similarity search (e.g. "only search docs tagged #docker").

## Project: Semantic Search Engine for Your Own Notes
Use **real data you already have** — your Notion learning notes or this very Obsidian vault once it has content.

**Requirements:**
- Ingest pipeline: read your markdown/Notion notes → chunk them → embed each chunk → store in Chroma (local, no server hassle)
- Query CLI or simple script: takes a natural-language query, returns top-k most relevant chunks with their source file
- Add metadata (filename, tags, date) and support filtering by it

**Stretch goal:** Add a second vector DB (Qdrant, running in Docker — you already know Docker) and benchmark query speed/quality against Chroma. This sets you up to make an informed choice in later RAG work instead of defaulting blindly.

**Stack:** Python, `chromadb`, an embeddings model (OpenAI `text-embedding-3-small` or a local one via `sentence-transformers`), Qdrant (stretch, via Docker)

